## The JIT Compiler and Native Code

When you run a .NET program, the CLR loads the assembly and starts executing it method by method. The first time a particular method is actually called, the **Just-In-Time (JIT) compiler** translates that method's IL into native machine code for the CPU the program is currently running on. That native code is then cached in memory, so every subsequent call to the same method reuses the already-compiled native version instead of re-translating the IL.

This is why .NET programs are often described as having a brief "warm-up" period: the very first call to a method is slightly slower because it pays the cost of JIT compilation, while later calls run at full native speed.

Modern .NET (Core 3.0+) uses **tiered compilation** to make this warm-up faster without sacrificing long-term performance. On first call, the JIT quickly produces an unoptimized "Tier 0" version of the method so the program can start responding immediately. If that method turns out to be called frequently (a hot path), the runtime recompiles it in the background into a heavily optimized "Tier 1" version and swaps it in transparently.

- JIT compilation happens per-method, on first use, not all at once at startup.
- Compiled native code is cached for the lifetime of the process.
- Tiered compilation trades a little peak performance early on for much faster startup.
- Alternatives like ReadyToRun and Native AOT move some or all of this work to build time instead of run time.

```csharp
static int Square(int x) => x * x;

for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
{
    Console.WriteLine(Square(i));
}
```

In the example above, `Square` is JIT-compiled to native code the first time the loop calls it; the remaining four calls reuse that same cached native code rather than being recompiled.

codeExamples.code:
int Square(int x) => x * x;

for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
{
    Console.WriteLine(Square(i));
}

interviewQuestions.answer:
The **CLR (Common Language Runtime)** is the overall execution engine for .NET programs. It is responsible for loading assemblies, managing memory and garbage collection, enforcing type safety, handling exceptions, managing threads, and coordinating everything needed to run a compiled .NET program.

The **JIT (Just-In-Time) compiler** is one specific component that runs inside the CLR. Its job is narrower: it translates a method's intermediate language (IL) into native machine code the first time that method is called, so the CPU can actually execute it.

In short, the JIT is a sub-system of the CLR -- the CLR is the whole runtime environment, and the JIT is the piece specifically responsible for turning IL into native instructions on demand.

```csharp
// The CLR loads this assembly and manages its execution.
// The JIT compiles Main's IL to native code the first time it runs.
Console.WriteLine("Managed by the CLR, compiled by the JIT");
```

Tiered compilation is a JIT strategy, enabled by default since .NET Core 3.0, that balances fast startup against peak runtime performance.

Without tiering, the JIT would have to choose between compiling methods quickly but with little optimization (fast startup, slower hot code) or compiling them slowly but heavily optimized (slow startup, fast hot code) for every single method, even ones only called once. Tiered compilation avoids this tradeoff by applying both strategies selectively:

- **Tier 0**: On a method's first call, the JIT emits a quick, minimally optimized version of the native code. This keeps application startup fast because no method pays a large compilation cost up front.
- **Tier 1**: The runtime monitors call counts in the background. If a method is called often enough to be considered "hot," it is recompiled with full optimizations (inlining, loop optimizations, better register allocation) and the optimized version transparently replaces the Tier 0 version for future calls.

This means rarely-called methods (like one-time startup code) never pay for expensive optimization they won't benefit from, while hot-path methods (like a request handler called thousands of times per second) eventually get fully optimized native code without blocking application startup.

```csharp
// Called once at startup -- stays at Tier 0, cheap to compile.
ConfigureLogging();

// Called on every request -- promoted to Tier 1 once it's identified as hot.
HandleRequest(request);
```

codeExamples.code:
using System.Reflection;

Assembly assembly = Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly();
Console.WriteLine(assembly.GetName().Name);

interviewQuestions.answer:
An assembly is the basic unit of deployment and versioning in .NET -- physically a `.dll` or `.exe` file produced by compiling a project. It contains three things bundled together: compiled IL code for every type and method in the project, metadata describing those types (names, members, attributes, visibility), and a manifest that identifies the assembly itself (name, version, and which other assemblies it references).

When you build a .NET project, the compiler emits exactly this kind of file into the `bin` folder. At run time, the CLR loads assemblies as needed -- your application's own assembly plus every assembly it depends on (its own code, third-party NuGet packages, and parts of the .NET runtime itself).

```csharp
// Compiling this file produces an assembly, e.g. MyApp.dll,
// containing the IL for Program and its Main method, plus metadata.
class Program
{
    static void Main() => Console.WriteLine("Hello!");
}
```

