## Strongly Typed Settings with the Options Pattern

Reading configuration through raw string keys (`config["Mail:MaxRetries"]`) works, but it's easy to typo a key, and there's no compile-time checking that the key actually exists or holds the right type. The **Options pattern** fixes this by binding a configuration section directly onto a plain C# class, giving you IntelliSense, type safety, and a single place where a setting's shape is defined.

You define a simple class matching the JSON shape, register it with `Configure<T>`, and then inject `IOptions<T>` wherever you need the values.

```csharp
public class MailOptions
{
    public string FromAddress { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public int MaxRetries { get; set; }
}

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.Configure<MailOptions>(builder.Configuration.GetSection("Mail"));

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapGet("/mail-info", (IOptions<MailOptions> options) =>
{
    var mail = options.Value;
    return $"{mail.FromAddress} (max {mail.MaxRetries} retries)";
});

app.Run();
```

There are three related interfaces, each suited to a different lifetime scenario:

- **`IOptions<T>`** -- resolved once and cached for the application's lifetime; it does not pick up changes made to the underlying configuration source after startup. Registerable as a singleton dependency.
- **`IOptionsSnapshot<T>`** -- recomputed once per request/scope, so it reflects configuration changes between requests in a scoped-lifetime-friendly way.
- **`IOptionsMonitor<T>`** -- a singleton-friendly type that can notify you of changes immediately via an `OnChange` callback, useful for background services that need to react to configuration changes without restarting.

Binding a whole class instead of scattering raw string-key lookups throughout your codebase also makes unit testing far easier -- you can construct an `Options.Create(new MailOptions { ... })` directly in a test, with no need to spin up a real configuration source at all.

codeExamples.code:
public class MailOptions
{
    public string FromAddress { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public int MaxRetries { get; set; }
}

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.Configure<MailOptions>(builder.Configuration.GetSection("Mail"));

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapGet("/mail-info", (IOptions<MailOptions> options) =>
{
    var mail = options.Value;
    return $"{mail.FromAddress} (max {mail.MaxRetries} retries)";
});

app.Run();

interviewQuestions.answer:
The Options pattern binds a section of configuration onto a plain C# class, then exposes that class through `IOptions<T>` (or its siblings `IOptionsSnapshot<T>` / `IOptionsMonitor<T>`) via dependency injection, instead of having consuming code read raw string keys out of `IConfiguration` directly.

```csharp
public class MailOptions
{
    public string FromAddress { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public int MaxRetries { get; set; }
}

builder.Services.Configure<MailOptions>(builder.Configuration.GetSection("Mail"));
```

It's preferred for several concrete reasons:

- **Type safety and IntelliSense**: `options.Value.MaxRetries` is a compiler-checked `int` property; `config["Mail:MaxRetries"]` is an untyped, unchecked string key that silently returns null if mistyped.
- **Single source of truth for shape**: the `MailOptions` class documents exactly what settings exist and their types, rather than that knowledge being scattered as magic strings across the codebase.
- **Testability**: a unit test can construct `Options.Create(new MailOptions { MaxRetries = 5 })` directly, with zero dependency on a real configuration file or `IConfiguration` instance at all.
- **Validation support**: you can attach data-annotation validation or custom validation logic (via `.ValidateDataAnnotations()` or `.Validate(...)`) that runs automatically when options are resolved, catching misconfiguration early.

Directly injecting `IConfiguration` is still reasonable for quick scripts, truly dynamic/ad-hoc key lookups, or configuration whose shape genuinely isn't known in advance, but for any settings with a known, stable shape, the Options pattern is the idiomatic approach in modern .NET.

All three expose the same bound options class, but they differ in lifetime and whether they react to configuration changes made after the application has started (for example, editing `appsettings.json` while `reloadOnChange: true` is set, or a config provider that supports live updates).

- **`IOptions<T>`**: resolved once, the first time it's requested, and cached for the rest of the application's lifetime. It never reflects changes made to configuration after that first resolution. It's registered with singleton lifetime semantics and is the simplest, lowest-overhead choice when settings genuinely never change while the app is running.
- **`IOptionsSnapshot<T>`**: recomputed once per scope (in ASP.NET Core, typically once per HTTP request). It reflects whatever the configuration source contains at the start of that scope, which makes it scoped-lifetime and unsuitable for injection into a singleton service (the DI container will throw, since a scoped service can't be captured by a singleton).
- **`IOptionsMonitor<T>`**: registered as a singleton but stays live -- calling `.CurrentValue` always returns the latest bound values, and you can also subscribe to an `.OnChange(...)` callback that fires whenever the underlying configuration changes. This makes it the right choice for long-lived singleton services (like a background worker) that need to react to configuration changes without being restarted.

```csharp
public class EmailWorker : BackgroundService
{
    private readonly IOptionsMonitor<MailOptions> _options;

    public EmailWorker(IOptionsMonitor<MailOptions> options)
    {
        _options = options;
        _options.OnChange(updated => Console.WriteLine($"Mail config changed: {updated.MaxRetries}"));
    }
}
```

Choosing between them comes down to: do you need live updates (`IOptionsMonitor`), do you need per-request freshness in a scoped context (`IOptionsSnapshot`), or is a one-time snapshot at startup good enough (`IOptions`)?

ASP.NET Core determines the current environment from the `ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT` environment variable (generic host-based apps outside ASP.NET Core use `DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT`), read once during host startup, before the configuration system is even fully built. If the variable isn't set, the default is `Production`.

The environment name is exposed through `IHostEnvironment.EnvironmentName`, and there are convenience extension methods like `IsDevelopment()`, `IsStaging()`, and `IsProduction()` for checking it in code (for example, enabling the developer exception page only in Development).

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
var app = builder.Build();

if (app.Environment.IsDevelopment())
{
    app.UseDeveloperExceptionPage();
}
```

The environment name also determines which `appsettings.{Environment}.json` overlay file gets loaded on top of the base `appsettings.json`.

The default host builds configuration from multiple providers added in a specific, fixed order: `appsettings.json`, then `appsettings.{Environment}.json`, then user secrets (Development only), then environment variables, then command-line arguments. Each provider is added *after* the ones before it, and when multiple providers define the same key, **the later provider wins** -- its value overrides anything set by an earlier provider for that exact key.

This ordering is deliberate: it goes from "most general, checked into source control" (`appsettings.json`) to "most specific to this particular run" (command-line arguments), so that a value you explicitly pass on the command line when launching the app always takes precedence over anything baked into a file, and an environment variable set by your hosting platform always overrides whatever the checked-in JSON files say.

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
// Equivalent to manually adding, in this order:
// builder.Configuration.AddJsonFile("appsettings.json")
//   .AddJsonFile($"appsettings.{env}.json", optional: true)
//   .AddUserSecrets(...)   // Development only
//   .AddEnvironmentVariables()
//   .AddCommandLine(args);
```

Practically, this means you can always override any setting for a one-off local run by passing it on the command line (`dotnet run --Mail:MaxRetries=10`), without touching any file, and a production deployment can always override a baked-in default via an environment variable set by its hosting platform, without rebuilding the application.

