## Working with List<T>

`List<T>` is the workhorse general-purpose collection in the Base Class Library (BCL). It's a dynamically resizable, ordered sequence of elements of type `T`, implemented internally as an array that automatically grows (typically doubling in capacity) whenever it fills up.

Unlike a plain array, `List<T>` doesn't require you to know the size up front: `Add` appends an element and transparently handles resizing, `Remove`/`RemoveAt` take elements out and shift later elements down, and `Contains` searches linearly for a value. Because it's generic, a `List<int>` stores `int` values directly with no boxing, and the compiler enforces that only `int` values can be added -- both a performance win and a type-safety win compared to the old non-generic `ArrayList`.

```csharp
var names = new List<string> { "Ada", "Grace", "Alan" };
names.Add("Linus");
names.Remove("Alan");

foreach (var name in names)
{
    Console.WriteLine(name);
}
```

- Indexing (`names[i]`) is O(1), just like an array, since the underlying storage is contiguous.
- `Add` is amortized O(1) -- occasionally O(n) when the internal array needs to grow and copy, but averaged over many additions it's constant time.
- `Contains`, `Remove`, and `IndexOf` are O(n), since they must scan the list looking for a match.
- Use `List<T>` when you need an ordered, index-accessible, resizable sequence and don't need fast key-based lookup.

If you already know the exact number of elements you'll store and that number won't change, a plain array can be marginally more efficient, but `List<T>` is the default choice for almost everything else involving a growing sequence of items.

codeExamples.code:
var names = new List<string> { "Ada", "Grace", "Alan" };
names.Add("Linus");
names.Remove("Alan");

foreach (var name in names)
{
    Console.WriteLine(name);
}

