## Registering Services in IServiceCollection

.NET ships a built-in dependency injection (DI) container, `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection`, used throughout ASP.NET Core and the generic host. Rather than classes constructing their own dependencies with `new`, you register implementations for interfaces up front, and the container builds and supplies them wherever they're needed.

`IServiceCollection` is the registration API, typically accessed through `builder.Services` in a minimal hosting model program. You register a mapping from an abstraction (usually an interface) to a concrete implementation using methods like `AddSingleton`, `AddScoped`, or `AddTransient` -- the exact method chosen controls the service's *lifetime*, covered in the next topic.

```csharp
public interface IGreeter
{
    string Greet(string name);
}

public class Greeter : IGreeter
{
    public string Greet(string name) => $"Hello, {name}!";
}

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IGreeter, Greeter>();

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapGet("/greet/{name}", (string name, IGreeter greeter) => greeter.Greet(name));

app.Run();
```

Notice that the minimal API handler simply declares an `IGreeter` parameter, and the framework supplies a `Greeter` instance automatically -- the handler never calls `new Greeter()` itself. This is the core benefit of DI: consuming code depends only on the abstraction, and the concrete implementation can be swapped (for testing, for a different environment, for a new version) by changing a single registration line, with no changes needed anywhere the interface is consumed.

- Register against interfaces (`AddSingleton<IGreeter, Greeter>()`) rather than concrete types, so consumers depend on an abstraction they can substitute later.
- Registration order generally doesn't matter for simple cases, but the *last* registration for the same service type wins if you register the same interface more than once (unless you specifically want to resolve all of them via `IEnumerable<T>`).
- Framework services (`ILogger<T>`, `IConfiguration`, `HttpClient` via `IHttpClientFactory`) are pre-registered for you by the hosting infrastructure.

codeExamples.code:
public interface IGreeter
{
    string Greet(string name);
}

public class Greeter : IGreeter
{
    public string Greet(string name) => $"Hello, {name}!";
}

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IGreeter, Greeter>();

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapGet("/greet/{name}", (string name, IGreeter greeter) => greeter.Greet(name));

app.Run();

