## How the Container Resolves Dependencies

Once `builder.Build()` runs, `IServiceCollection` is compiled into an `IServiceProvider` -- the actual runtime container that resolves services on demand. Understanding how resolution works clarifies a lot of DI behavior that otherwise seems like magic.

When something asks the container for a service -- either explicitly via `provider.GetRequiredService<T>()`, or implicitly because it's a constructor parameter of another service being resolved -- the container looks at that type's registration, inspects its constructor, and recursively resolves every constructor parameter the same way, building a complete dependency graph bottom-up before finally constructing the requested object.

```csharp
var services = new ServiceCollection();
services.AddTransient<IGreeter, Greeter>();

using ServiceProvider provider = services.BuildServiceProvider();
IGreeter greeter = provider.GetRequiredService<IGreeter>();

Console.WriteLine(greeter.Greet("Grace"));
```

This example builds a standalone `ServiceProvider` directly (useful for console apps, tests, or understanding DI outside of ASP.NET Core) and resolves `IGreeter` manually with `GetRequiredService<T>()`, which throws an `InvalidOperationException` if no registration exists, as opposed to `GetService<T>()`, which returns `null` in that case.

- The container only knows how to resolve constructor parameters whose types are themselves registered -- an unregistered dependency causes resolution to fail with a clear exception naming the missing type.
- `GetRequiredService<T>()` throws if the service isn't registered; `GetService<T>()` returns null instead, which is useful when a dependency is genuinely optional.
- In ASP.NET Core, you almost never call `GetRequiredService` yourself -- the framework resolves constructor parameters (for controllers, minimal API handlers, middleware) automatically behind the scenes.
- Calling `BuildServiceProvider()` yourself, as in this standalone example, is mainly useful for console apps and tests; in ASP.NET Core, `builder.Build()` already does this for you once.

codeExamples.code:
var services = new ServiceCollection();
services.AddTransient<IGreeter, Greeter>();

using ServiceProvider provider = services.BuildServiceProvider();
IGreeter greeter = provider.GetRequiredService<IGreeter>();

Console.WriteLine(greeter.Greet("Grace"));

interviewQuestions.answer:
Dependency injection (DI) is a design pattern where a class declares the dependencies it needs (usually as constructor parameters, typed as interfaces) rather than creating those dependencies itself with `new`. Something external -- a DI container, or just a human wiring things up in tests -- supplies ("injects") the actual implementation at the point where the class is constructed.

```csharp
// Without DI: OrderService creates and owns its dependency directly
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly SqlEmailSender _emailSender = new SqlEmailSender();
}

// With DI: OrderService depends on an abstraction, supplied from outside
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly IEmailSender _emailSender;
    public OrderService(IEmailSender emailSender) => _emailSender = emailSender;
}
```

It solves two main problems. First, **testability**: a class that depends on an abstraction can be tested with a fake/mock implementation instead of a real one (a real database, a real email server), without changing the class itself. Second, **loose coupling and flexibility**: because consuming code depends only on an interface, you can change the concrete implementation (swap one email provider for another, add caching, change environments) by updating a single registration, with zero changes to every class that uses that interface.

.NET's built-in DI container (`Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection`) automates the "supplying" part: you register interface-to-implementation mappings once at startup, and the container constructs the entire object graph automatically whenever something needs a registered service.

By default, when running in the Development environment, ASP.NET Core's host automatically enables two validation checks on the service provider, both configurable via `ServiceProviderOptions`:

- **`ValidateScopes`**: catches *captive dependency* bugs by throwing an exception if code tries to resolve a scoped service from the root container (rather than from within a request/operation-scoped `IServiceScope`), or if a singleton service is constructed with a scoped service injected into its constructor. Without this check, such a mistake can silently "work" by capturing a scoped instance for the entire lifetime of the singleton, causing subtle bugs like a database context being reused across unrelated requests.
- **`ValidateOnBuild`**: eagerly walks the entire registered dependency graph once, right when `BuildServiceProvider()` (or `builder.Build()`) runs, and throws immediately if any registered service's constructor dependencies can't actually be satisfied -- rather than waiting until that specific service happens to be resolved for the first time, possibly deep into production traffic.

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Explicitly configuring these (they're already on by default in Development)
builder.Host.UseDefaultServiceProvider((context, options) =>
{
    options.ValidateScopes = true;
    options.ValidateOnBuild = true;
});
```

Both checks are enabled by default only in Development (for performance reasons -- the extra validation work isn't free) but can be explicitly turned on in any environment, including Production, if you want the stronger safety guarantee at the cost of a small amount of extra startup time. Many teams explicitly enable both in all environments precisely because catching a captive-dependency or missing-registration bug at application startup is far preferable to discovering it as an intermittent production incident.

