## Singleton, Scoped, and Transient Explained

When registering a service, you choose one of three lifetimes, each controlling how long a constructed instance is reused before the container creates a new one.

- **Singleton** (`AddSingleton`): exactly one instance is created for the entire lifetime of the application, and that same instance is returned for every subsequent resolution, from any part of the app.
- **Scoped** (`AddScoped`): one instance is created per *scope* -- in ASP.NET Core, a new scope is automatically created for each incoming HTTP request, so a scoped service is shared by everything resolved during that one request, but a fresh instance is created for the next request.
- **Transient** (`AddTransient`): a brand new instance is created every single time the service is resolved, even multiple times within the same request or scope.

```csharp
public class OperationId
{
    public Guid Id { get; } = Guid.NewGuid();
}

var services = new ServiceCollection();
services.AddSingleton<OperationId>();

using var provider = services.BuildServiceProvider();

var first = provider.GetRequiredService<OperationId>();
var second = provider.GetRequiredService<OperationId>();

Console.WriteLine(first.Id == second.Id);
```

Because `OperationId` is registered as a singleton, both `GetRequiredService` calls return the *same* constructed instance -- so `first.Id` and `second.Id` are literally the same `Guid`, generated exactly once, when the singleton was first created.

- Use **singleton** for stateless services, or services holding genuinely application-wide state/caches that are safe to share across every request and thread.
- Use **scoped** for anything tied to a single logical operation, most commonly EF Core's `DbContext`, which should not be shared across unrelated requests.
- Use **transient** for lightweight, stateless services where creating a new instance each time is cheap and there's no reason to share state.
- Choosing the wrong lifetime is one of the most common sources of subtle DI bugs -- particularly injecting a shorter-lived service into a longer-lived one.

codeExamples.code:
public class OperationId
{
    public Guid Id { get; } = Guid.NewGuid();
}

var services = new ServiceCollection();
services.AddSingleton<OperationId>();

using var provider = services.BuildServiceProvider();

var first = provider.GetRequiredService<OperationId>();
var second = provider.GetRequiredService<OperationId>();

Console.WriteLine(first.Id == second.Id);

