## The Captive Dependency Problem

A **captive dependency** happens when a longer-lived service (typically a singleton) holds a reference to a shorter-lived service (scoped or transient) by capturing it through constructor injection. Because the singleton is only constructed once, whatever scoped instance it captures at that moment gets held onto for the application's *entire* lifetime -- defeating the whole point of that dependency being scoped (fresh per request) in the first place.

This is dangerous specifically because, depending on configuration, the container might not catch it automatically: with `ValidateScopes` disabled, registering a singleton that takes a scoped service as a constructor parameter can silently "work" at first resolution, while actually causing one request's scoped instance (say, a database context) to be reused across every subsequent request for the life of the application -- a serious and hard-to-diagnose bug.

The correct way for a singleton to use scoped functionality is to inject `IServiceScopeFactory` instead of the scoped service directly, and create a *new* scope each time it actually needs to use that functionality:

```csharp
public class ScopedCounter
{
    public int Value { get; set; }
}

public class SingletonWorker
{
    private readonly IServiceScopeFactory _scopeFactory;

    public SingletonWorker(IServiceScopeFactory scopeFactory)
    {
        _scopeFactory = scopeFactory;
    }

    public int GetScopedValue()
    {
        using IServiceScope scope = _scopeFactory.CreateScope();
        var counter = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ScopedCounter>();
        counter.Value = 42;
        return counter.Value;
    }
}

var services = new ServiceCollection();
services.AddScoped<ScopedCounter>();
services.AddSingleton<SingletonWorker>();

using var provider = services.BuildServiceProvider();
var worker = provider.GetRequiredService<SingletonWorker>();

Console.WriteLine(worker.GetScopedValue());
```

Here, `SingletonWorker` never captures a `ScopedCounter` in its own constructor. Instead, each call to `GetScopedValue()` creates a brand-new, short-lived scope, resolves a fresh `ScopedCounter` from *that* scope, uses it, and then disposes the scope -- correctly getting a new scoped instance every time, with no captivity.

- Captive dependencies most commonly show up as a singleton (e.g., a `BackgroundService`) directly injecting a scoped `DbContext`.
- `IServiceScopeFactory` is itself registered as a singleton by the container, so it's always safe to inject into another singleton.
- `ValidateScopes` (on by default in Development) catches the most direct form of this bug at resolution time, but doesn't catch every possible variation, so understanding the underlying rule is still essential.

codeExamples.code:
public class ScopedCounter
{
    public int Value { get; set; }
}

public class SingletonWorker
{
    private readonly IServiceScopeFactory _scopeFactory;

    public SingletonWorker(IServiceScopeFactory scopeFactory)
    {
        _scopeFactory = scopeFactory;
    }

    public int GetScopedValue()
    {
        using IServiceScope scope = _scopeFactory.CreateScope();
        var counter = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ScopedCounter>();
        counter.Value = 42;
        return counter.Value;
    }
}

var services = new ServiceCollection();
services.AddScoped<ScopedCounter>();
services.AddSingleton<SingletonWorker>();

using var provider = services.BuildServiceProvider();
var worker = provider.GetRequiredService<SingletonWorker>();

Console.WriteLine(worker.GetScopedValue());

