## Idiomatic Constructor Injection

Constructor injection is the dominant, recommended way to receive dependencies in .NET: a class declares every dependency it needs as a constructor parameter, typed against an interface, assigns each one to a `private readonly` field, and uses those fields throughout the rest of the class. The DI container supplies the actual implementations automatically whenever it constructs the class.

```csharp
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly IClock _clock;
    private readonly ILogger<OrderService> _logger;

    public OrderService(IClock clock, ILogger<OrderService> logger)
    {
        _clock = clock ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(clock));
        _logger = logger ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(logger));
    }

    public string PlaceOrder(string item)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Placing order for {Item}", item);
        return $"{item} ordered at {_clock.UtcNow:O}";
    }
}
```

C# 12 introduced **primary constructors**, which let you fold the constructor parameter list directly onto the class declaration, eliminating the boilerplate of separately declared fields and a hand-written constructor body just to assign them:

```csharp
public class OrderService(IClock clock, ILogger<OrderService> logger)
{
    public string PlaceOrder(string item)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Placing order for {Item}", item);
        return $"{item} ordered at {clock.UtcNow:O}";
    }
}
```

Both versions are functionally equivalent from the DI container's perspective -- it still sees a single public constructor and resolves `IClock` and `ILogger<OrderService>` for it the same way. The primary-constructor version is simply far less boilerplate for the common case of "accept some dependencies, store them, use them."

- Mark fields `readonly` to make it clear (and compiler-enforced) that a dependency is never reassigned after construction.
- Guard clauses (`?? throw new ArgumentNullException(...)`) are optional with DI, since the container always supplies a non-null registered instance, but remain good defensive practice for classes that might also be constructed manually (e.g., in tests).
- A class should have exactly one constructor that DI uses to resolve it; if multiple public constructors exist, you must tell the container which one to prefer (or avoid the ambiguity by having just one).

codeExamples.code:
public interface IClock { DateTime UtcNow { get; } }
public class SystemClock : IClock { public DateTime UtcNow => DateTime.UtcNow; }

// Primary constructor: parameters declared directly on the class
public class OrderService(IClock clock, ILogger<OrderService> logger)
{
    public string PlaceOrder(string item)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Placing order for {Item}", item);
        return $"{item} ordered at {clock.UtcNow:O}";
    }
}

