## What Is a Task?

A `Task` represents an operation that may not have finished yet -- a "promise" of a result (or just of completion, for a non-generic `Task`) that will eventually become available. `Task<TResult>` is the generic counterpart that additionally carries a result value of type `TResult` once it completes. This is the foundational type that nearly everything in .NET's asynchronous programming model -- including `async`/`await` -- is built on top of.

A task can be in one of several states: not yet started, running, completed successfully, faulted (an exception occurred), or canceled. You can create and start a task explicitly with `Task.Run`, which queues a delegate to run on a thread-pool thread, or you get one implicitly by calling an `async` method, which returns a `Task`/`Task<TResult>` representing that method's eventual completion.

```csharp
Task<int> task = Task.Run(() =>
{
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i <= 100; i++)
    {
        sum += i;
    }
    return sum;
});

int result = await task;
Console.WriteLine(result);
```

Awaiting a task (`await task`) suspends the current method until the task completes, then yields its result (or re-throws its exception, if it faulted) -- without blocking the calling thread while waiting.

- `Task` represents an operation with no return value; `Task<TResult>` represents one that produces a value of type `TResult`.
- `Task.Run` is specifically for offloading CPU-bound work onto a thread-pool thread; it is not the right tool for I/O-bound work, which should use a naturally asynchronous API (an `async` method provided by the library you're calling) instead.
- A task that has already completed can still be awaited -- `await` on an already-finished task returns its result immediately, without any actual suspension.
- Tasks are a one-time construct: once a task completes, awaiting it again just returns the same cached result (or re-throws the same exception) rather than re-running anything.

codeExamples.code:
Task<int> task = Task.Run(() =>
{
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i <= 100; i++)
    {
        sum += i;
    }
    return sum;
});

int result = await task;
Console.WriteLine(result);

