## Writing Your First xUnit Test

**xUnit** is one of the most widely used unit testing frameworks for .NET. A test project is just an ordinary class library with a reference to the `xunit` and `xunit.runner.visualstudio` NuGet packages (scaffolded automatically with `dotnet new xunit`), containing test classes whose methods are marked with attributes that the test runner recognizes.

The simplest kind of test method is marked `[Fact]` -- a test that always runs the same way, with no parameters, checking one specific scenario. Inside a fact, you typically follow the **Arrange-Act-Assert** pattern: set up whatever the test needs (Arrange), perform the action being tested (Act), then verify the outcome (Assert) using one of xUnit's `Assert` methods.

```csharp
using Xunit;

public class CalculatorTests
{
    [Fact]
    public void Add_ReturnsSum_WhenGivenTwoPositiveNumbers()
    {
        var calculator = new Calculator();

        int result = calculator.Add(2, 3);

        Assert.Equal(5, result);
    }
}

public class Calculator
{
    public int Add(int a, int b) => a + b;
}
```

`Assert.Equal(expected, actual)` compares two values and fails the test (throwing an exception that the test runner catches and reports) if they aren't equal; xUnit provides many other assertion methods (`Assert.True`, `Assert.NotNull`, `Assert.Throws<TException>`, `Assert.Contains`, and more) covering the vast majority of things a test needs to check.

- Test method names commonly follow a `MethodUnderTest_ExpectedBehavior_Condition` convention (as in `Add_ReturnsSum_WhenGivenTwoPositiveNumbers`), making failing test names self-explanatory in a test report.
- xUnit creates a brand-new instance of the test class for *every* test method, so tests don't accidentally share mutable state through instance fields -- a deliberate design choice for test isolation.
- Running `dotnet test` in a project (or solution) directory discovers and runs every `[Fact]`/`[Theory]` test it can find.

codeExamples.code:
using Xunit;

public class CalculatorTests
{
    [Fact]
    public void Add_ReturnsSum_WhenGivenTwoPositiveNumbers()
    {
        var calculator = new Calculator();

        int result = calculator.Add(2, 3);

        Assert.Equal(5, result);
    }
}

public class Calculator
{
    public int Add(int a, int b) => a + b;
}

lessons.contentMarkdown:
## Parameterized Tests with Theory and InlineData

Writing a separate `[Fact]` for every input/output combination you want to check quickly becomes repetitive. xUnit's `[Theory]` attribute lets a single test method run multiple times, once for each set of inputs you supply, with `[InlineData(...)]` being the simplest way to provide those inputs directly in the test's source code.

```csharp
using Xunit;

public class CalculatorTests
{
    [Theory]
    [InlineData(2, 3, 5)]
    [InlineData(-1, 1, 0)]
    [InlineData(0, 0, 0)]
    public void Add_ReturnsExpectedSum(int a, int b, int expected)
    {
        var calculator = new Calculator();

        int result = calculator.Add(a, b);

        Assert.Equal(expected, result);
    }
}
```

Each `[InlineData(...)]` attribute supplies one row of arguments matching the test method's parameter list; xUnit runs the method once per row, reporting each run as its own separate pass/fail result in test output (so a failure in one row doesn't hide whether the other rows passed). This keeps the actual test logic written exactly once, while still thoroughly exercising several distinct input combinations, including edge cases like negative numbers or zero.

For data that's more complex than simple literal values (objects, computed values, data shared across multiple theories), xUnit also supports `[MemberData]` (pointing at a static property or method that yields the test cases) and `[ClassData]` (pointing at a class that implements `IEnumerable<object[]>`), both of which scale better than `InlineData` once the data itself needs real logic to produce.

- `[InlineData]` values must be compile-time constants (literals), since they're embedded as attribute arguments.
- Each `[InlineData]` row runs as an independently reported test case, which is useful for seeing exactly which specific input combination failed, if any.
- Favor `Theory`+`InlineData` whenever you find yourself copy-pasting near-identical `[Fact]` methods that only differ in their literal input/expected values.

codeExamples.code:
using Xunit;

public class CalculatorTests
{
    [Theory]
    [InlineData(2, 3, 5)]
    [InlineData(-1, 1, 0)]
    [InlineData(0, 0, 0)]
    public void Add_ReturnsExpectedSum(int a, int b, int expected)
    {
        var calculator = new Calculator();

        int result = calculator.Add(a, b);

        Assert.Equal(expected, result);
    }
}

interviewQuestions.answer:
`[Fact]` marks a test that always runs the same way, with no parameters -- it checks exactly one specific scenario, and either passes or fails as a single result.

`[Theory]` marks a parameterized test: the test method takes parameters, and you supply one or more sets of input values (most simply, via `[InlineData(...)]` attributes), and xUnit runs the method once per supplied data set, reporting each run as its own separate result.

```csharp
[Fact]
public void IsEven_ReturnsTrue_ForTwo()
{
    Assert.True(NumberHelpers.IsEven(2));
}

[Theory]
[InlineData(2, true)]
[InlineData(3, false)]
[InlineData(0, true)]
public void IsEven_ReturnsExpectedResult(int number, bool expected)
{
    Assert.Equal(expected, NumberHelpers.IsEven(number));
}
```

In short: use `[Fact]` for a single, fixed check, and `[Theory]` when you want to run the same test logic against multiple different inputs without duplicating the method body for each one.

Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) is a simple, widely followed structure for organizing the body of a unit test into three clearly separated sections, making tests easier to read and reason about regardless of who wrote them.

- **Arrange**: set up everything the test needs -- construct the object under test, prepare input values, configure any fakes/mocks for its dependencies.
- **Act**: perform the single action actually being tested -- typically one method call.
- **Assert**: verify the outcome matches what's expected, using assertion methods that fail the test if the check doesn't hold.

```csharp
[Fact]
public void Add_ReturnsSum_WhenGivenTwoPositiveNumbers()
{
    // Arrange
    var calculator = new Calculator();

    // Act
    int result = calculator.Add(2, 3);

    // Assert
    Assert.Equal(5, result);
}
```

Following this structure consistently makes it immediately obvious, at a glance, what a test is setting up, what it's actually exercising, and what it's checking -- which matters a great deal when a test fails months later and someone unfamiliar with it needs to quickly understand what broke. It also tends to discourage testing multiple unrelated behaviors in a single test method, since cramming more than one "Act" into a test usually signals it should be split into separate, more focused tests.

When a class under test depends on an interface (say, `IOrderRepository`) rather than a concrete database-backed implementation, you can supply a **test double** -- a fake, stub, or mock implementation of that interface -- instead of the real one, so the test exercises only the logic of the class under test, with no actual database, network call, or other external dependency involved.

The simplest approach is writing a small hand-rolled fake class that implements the interface with in-memory behavior:

```csharp
public class FakeOrderRepository : IOrderRepository
{
    private readonly List<Order> _orders = new();
    public void Add(Order order) => _orders.Add(order);
    public Order? GetById(int id) => _orders.FirstOrDefault(o => o.Id == id);
}

[Fact]
public void PlaceOrder_SavesOrderToRepository()
{
    var repository = new FakeOrderRepository();
    var service = new OrderService(repository);

    service.PlaceOrder(new Order { Id = 1, Item = "Widget" });

    Assert.NotNull(repository.GetById(1));
}
```

For more complex interfaces, a mocking library like **Moq** or **NSubstitute** lets you dynamically generate an implementation at test time, configuring exactly how specific method calls should behave (what to return, what exception to throw) and optionally verifying that specific methods were called with expected arguments, without writing a full hand-rolled fake class yourself:

```csharp
var mockRepository = new Mock<IOrderRepository>();
mockRepository.Setup(r => r.GetById(1)).Returns(new Order { Id = 1 });

var service = new OrderService(mockRepository.Object);
var order = service.GetOrder(1);

Assert.Equal(1, order.Id);
mockRepository.Verify(r => r.GetById(1), Times.Once);
```

This is precisely why constructor injection against interfaces (rather than concrete classes) matters so much for testability: because `OrderService` depends on `IOrderRepository` rather than a specific database-backed class, nothing about the test needs a real database connection, and the test runs fast and deterministically, every time, in any environment.

