## Log Levels and Providers

Logging in .NET is governed by two related, independently configurable concepts: **log levels**, which rank the severity/verbosity of individual log entries, and **providers**, which determine where those entries actually end up (console output, a debug window, a file, a centralized logging service).

The `LogLevel` enum, from least to most severe, is: `Trace`, `Debug`, `Information`, `Warning`, `Error`, `Critical` (plus a special `None` value used only to disable logging for a category entirely). Each category (typically a class's full name, as set by `ILogger<T>`'s generic parameter) has a configurable *minimum* level -- log calls below that threshold for that category are silently discarded and never reach any provider at all.

```json
{
  "Logging": {
    "LogLevel": {
      "Default": "Information",
      "Microsoft.AspNetCore": "Warning"
    }
  }
}
```

This configuration sets the default minimum level to `Information` for every category, but raises the threshold specifically for anything under the `Microsoft.AspNetCore` category to `Warning` -- meaning routine framework-level request-handling logs (which the framework logs at `Information` by default) get suppressed, while your own application code's `Information`-level logs still come through, and anything at `Warning` or above from either source still appears.

The minimum level threshold is checked *before* any provider sees the log entry at all -- it's a single gate that applies regardless of how many providers are configured. Once a log entry passes that threshold, it's then sent to every configured provider simultaneously: the default template includes Console and Debug providers, and most real applications add a dedicated structured-logging provider (Serilog, NLog, Application Insights, or OpenTelemetry-based exporters) for production use, often routing logs to a centralized, searchable log store rather than relying solely on console output.

- Log level filtering is configured per category, with more specific category prefixes overriding the `Default` entry for anything under that prefix.
- The same log entry, once it passes its category's minimum level check, goes to every active provider -- you don't configure the level separately per provider by default, though some providers do support their own additional filtering.
- `Trace` and `Debug` are typically only enabled during active local development or deep troubleshooting, since they're extremely verbose and would otherwise flood production logs with low-value detail.

codeExamples.code:
{
  "Logging": {
    "LogLevel": {
      "Default": "Information",
      "Microsoft.AspNetCore": "Warning"
    }
  }
}

interviewQuestions.answer:
`ILogger<T>` is .NET's built-in, provider-agnostic logging interface. The generic parameter `T` (almost always the class doing the logging) is used purely to automatically tag every log entry from that logger with `T`'s full type name as its "category" -- useful later for filtering or configuring verbosity per category.

You get an instance of it the same way you get any other registered service: through dependency injection, typically as a constructor parameter (or, in a minimal API handler, as a parameter directly). The hosting infrastructure automatically registers logging services for you, so no manual `AddLogging()` call or similar registration is usually needed just to start using it.

```csharp
public class OrderService(ILogger<OrderService> logger)
{
    public void PlaceOrder(string item)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Placing order for {Item}", item);
    }
}
```

Because it's resolved through the same DI container as everything else, you never need to construct a logger yourself with `new` -- the container supplies a correctly configured instance, already wired up to whatever logging providers (console, debug, third-party sinks) the application has configured.

Writing `logger.LogInformation("User {UserId} logged in", userId)` uses a **message template** with a named placeholder and passes the actual value as a separate argument, rather than pre-combining them into one string via interpolation (`logger.LogInformation($"User {userId} logged in")`). This distinction is the core of **structured logging**, and it matters for reasons beyond just style.

When you pass the template and value separately, the logging provider receives both the literal template text *and* the raw, typed value of `userId` as independent pieces of data. A structured-logging-capable provider (Serilog, Application Insights, most centralized log aggregation systems) can then store `UserId` as its own distinct, indexed, queryable field -- letting you later search or filter logs with something like `UserId = 42`, directly and reliably.

```csharp
// Structured: UserId is captured as its own queryable field
logger.LogInformation("User {UserId} logged in", userId);

// Interpolated: the value is baked into one opaque string -- only text search is possible
logger.LogInformation($"User {userId} logged in");
```

With string interpolation, the value is irreversibly flattened into the final message text before the logger ever sees it -- there's no way for any downstream tool to recover `userId` as a distinct field; you're reduced to fragile text pattern matching against the full message string if you ever need to search or aggregate by that value later. There's also a smaller performance consideration: `LogInformation` checks the configured minimum log level *before* doing any of this formatting work, so if the configured level would discard the message anyway, the template version avoids the cost of building a string at all -- whereas a `$"..."` interpolated string is always fully constructed up front, regardless of whether the log level would have discarded it.

The `Logging:LogLevel` section in configuration controls a single, provider-independent filtering gate based on a log entry's **category** (typically the full type name passed as `ILogger<T>`'s generic parameter). Before a log call even reaches any provider, the logging infrastructure checks whether that category's effective minimum level permits the call's severity -- if not, the entire log call is discarded immediately, and *no* provider ever sees it, regardless of how many providers are configured.

```json
{
  "Logging": {
    "LogLevel": {
      "Default": "Information",
      "Microsoft.AspNetCore": "Warning",
      "MyApp.OrderService": "Debug"
    }
  }
}
```

The effective minimum level for a given category is resolved by finding the most specific matching prefix: a log entry from `MyApp.OrderService` matches that exact, most-specific entry (`Debug`), one from `MyApp.PaymentService` falls back to `Default` (`Information`), since there's no more specific override, and one from `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Routing` matches the `Microsoft.AspNetCore` prefix (`Warning`).

Once a log entry passes this single category-level gate, it is then dispatched to *every currently registered provider* -- Console, Debug, and any third-party providers like Serilog sinks -- simultaneously; there is no separate, provider-specific minimum level in the basic configuration model by default (though some providers, including Serilog's own configuration system when used as the primary logging pipeline, do support an additional layer of provider-specific or sink-specific level filtering beyond this shared gate). This means lowering the `Default` level to `Debug` to get more verbose console output during local troubleshooting will, by default, also send that same flood of Debug-level entries to every other active provider, including a production log aggregation sink, unless that provider has its own additional filtering configured to constrain it independently.
